C keyword 'restrict'
    This qualifier can be used for a pointer type to make a promise
      the object @OBJ pointed by pointer @P has no any other way
      can access to it that bypass P.
    A restricted pointer,can not points to the object which has
      automatic storage-age
      static linkage
    Thus,we can only assign an address of an object to the restricted
    pointer that must be heap-allocated.
    The primary usage for this qualifier is in function's parameters
    list,where 'restrict' is used to qualify the pointer type parameters.
      int foo(int *restrict a, int *restrict b)
      {
        *a = 5;
        *b = 6;
        return *a + *b;
      }

      movq    $0x05, (%rdi)  # *a = 5
      movq    $0x06, (%rsi)  # *b = 6

      # movq    (%rdi), %rax <= removed because restrict optimization
      # addq    $0x06, %rax  <= removed because restrict optimization
    
      movq    $0x0b, %rax    # a + b
      ret

    ! Compiler can freely to ignore 'restrict' qualifier if necessary.
      If the promise made by 'restrict' keyword is broken later,
      the execution would be undefined.
      # so we usually need to limit the scope for restricted pointers.